      into main.tsx. vite build compiled all 8 into a live mount (Q1-Q5 and Q7 onto the shared grid; Q6 onto a SECOND
      instance of the grid module; Q8 onto the shared grid). The guard stayed 81/81 green.
      - Q1/Q2 (the wrapper called as a plain function): outside the wrapper, every import of the wrapper module, by any
        path-like spelling (./, ../, @/, /src/, with or without a script extension, any letter case), binds names that
        may appear only as a JSX tag, after typeof, or as a member name. This is the verifier's fix (a). In such a
        file, cloneElement, createElement and \u are refused. A namespace import, a re-export, an unattributed import
        (string-named or side-effect) and a dynamic import()/require() of the wrapper module are refused too.
        GridWidgetBody.tsx needed comment-only rewording: its JSDoc named the wrapper three times outside a tag, and
        comments are scanned as code.
      - Q3/Q4 (a carrier beside src/, or in a __* / test path): this is the verifier's fix (b). A specifier that names
        the shared library must resolve into the library's src, directly or through the npm link. It may not resolve
        into a __* segment or a .test/.spec/.stories file, with or without the extension. The fan-in fact also gained
        a rule (SharedEscapes): every shipped library file, the allowed carriers included, is refused if it loads a
        module outside src/ or from a test / __* path. Without it, a shipped file could re-export a __* carrier past
        both checks. Q4 class: the fan-in's test exclusion is now sound, because nothing shipped can reach a test path.
      - Q5 (@spaarke/ui-components/../...): this is the verifier's fix (c). The alias is now resolved to the
        library's src, then checked by the same rule. It is no longer trusted by prefix.
      - Q6 (found here): import Table from '@spaarke/ui-components/components/DataGrid/DataGrid?v=1'. Vite loads a
        second module instance, and the default-export rule read the last segment 'DataGrid?v=1' as a non-grid name.
        A ?query or #fragment in any specifier is now refused (static, side-effect and dynamic).
      - Q7 (found here): import { Grid } from 'react-window/../../zzoutside/gridAlias2'. react-window is a declared
        package with no exports map, so the subpath stepped out to any file. A bare specifier with a '.' or '..'
        segment is now refused.
      - Q8 (found here, NOT closed, documented residual 5): element-tree peeling with no wrapper reference. In a file
        that never names the wrapper: const outer = (createGridWidgetBody('x') as any)({}).props.children; then
        outer.type(outer.props).type is the shared grid, mounted with the picker on. It compiled. No fixed spelling
        identifies it (computed keys, Object.values, a helper in another file). Refusing every .type read would refuse
        ordinary code (event.type, file.type). No wrapper in the same JavaScript realm can hide the element it must
        hand to React; every layer peels the same way. Residual 5, previously "React-internals fiber walk", is widened
        to this class, with the compiled example. The BFF allow-list still returns 400 for every internal-view column.
      Proof the guard bites, all re-seeded in this worktree and wired into main.tsx for the build:
      - With the fix, the production fact went red on exactly Q1-Q7: Q1 (cloneElement + non-JSX reference), Q2, Q3,
        Q4, Q5, Q6 and Q7. Q8 was not flagged; it is the residual.
      - SharedEscapes seeds: a new shipped file components/ZzSeed/ZzSeed.ts re-exporting from ../../../zzoutside and
        ../DataGrid/__zz__ made the fan-in fact red with 2 entries. A line appended to the allowed carrier src/index.ts
        (export from ../zzoutside) made it red with 1 entry, which shows the allowed carriers are not exempt.
      - Every seed was deleted. main.tsx and src/index.ts were restored with git checkout and touched. The npm-touched
        package-lock.json was reverted.
      MINOR item 2 (overclaims): CLOSED.
      - ExternalDataGrid.tsx JSDoc, which is pinned: it now says the runtime guard covers every JSX mount (caller prop,
        spread, clone or re-creation of an ExternalDataGrid ELEMENT) and does NOT protect the element it returns when
        called as a function. That is why the guard refuses any non-JSX use, and peeling is a residual.
