    <outcome date="2026-10-02" branch="task/uac-r2-157-b2-r2" round="review-6-fix-b2-r2" base="e205c1b7e">
      Status stays "completed-with-escalation". Two items stay open: the MANUAL live gate (criterion 6, pending by
      design; deploy order unchanged, below) and the owner decision on F1 (note section 8).
      BLOCKING item 1 / criterion 1 (R1-R5 compiled into live picker-on mounts; the guard stayed 109/109 green):
      the five shapes are now refused, each with a Theory row and a seeded production run. The scan is still not a
      proof; residuals 1-5 stand, and residual 2 now names the tsconfig / React-plugin JSX options.
      - R1-R3 (item 2-4, a comment between a module keyword and its specifier or "("): a comment after 'from',
        'import' or 'require' that is followed by a quote or "(" is refused (CommentAfterModuleKeyword). The verifier
        offered two fixes; I took the refusal, not "treat comments as whitespace in every specifier regex". Reason:
        inside JSX children "/*" is TEXT, so a specifier regex that skips a "comment" can be made to skip live code,
        and in the regexes whose matches are attributed (parsed import spans) or excluded (importSpans for the
        reference rule) a skipped span would hide what it covers. Worked through by hand, not compiled: a 'from'
        in JSX text followed by "/*" would let a comment-skipping AnyFrom match run to the next "*/" and swallow a real
        import in between (regex matches do not overlap). A refusal rule has no such failure, because every match is
        already a violation. The comment bounds are JavaScript's (a block ends at the first */, a line at \n, \r,
        U+2028 or U+2029; the R3i row covers U+2028). A comment INSIDE import( ... ) already failed the plain-literal
        rule (R3c row).
      - R4 (item 5, a JSX pragma): @jsx in any form (@jsx, @jsxImportSource, @jsxRuntime, @jsxFrag; case-insensitive)
        is refused anywhere in an external-SPA file, comment or not.
      - R5 (item 6, src/node_modules): a specifier that resolves into a node_modules folder inside the SPA source,
        or inside the library's src, is refused. The production facts also fail if any node_modules folder exists
        under external-spa/src or Spaarke.UI.Components/src, because the scans skip node_modules AND Vite resolves
        even a DECLARED bare name (react) through the nearest node_modules walking up from the importer. The
        verifier's "scan src/**/node_modules" alternative would not cover that bare-name route.
      - Item 7, the shared library: SharedEscapes (always reported, carriers included) now refuses a comment or
        U+FEFF between a module keyword and a specifier or argument that could load a library module (relative,
        root-relative or alias path, or text naming the library or DataGrid), and any JSX pragma. The library rule is
        narrower than the SPA's on purpose: a first form (keyword, then any comment) had at least 24 false positives in the
        library (prose ending a comment line with "from" or "import" above another comment line); the final SPA form
        (comment, then a quote or "(") still had one (surfaceLaunchRegistry.ts:161: prose ending in "from", a comment
        line, then the quoted object key 'nda-review'). A bare package there is residual 1 already. The library is
        not refused a literal U+FEFF globally either: csvExport.ts:32 holds the UTF-8 BOM as a string on purpose. The regex is a lookahead
        from each keyword, so a misread "comment" cannot swallow a later keyword. SharedEscapes reads AnyFrom /
        SideEffectImport / DynamicCall (whitespace-only) for its path rules, so the new rule is what covers the gap.
      - Found while fixing, same class, each compile-proven with vite build in this worktree and then refused:
        (a) U+FEFF between 'from' and the specifier: JavaScript whitespace, not .NET \s. Seed compiled to
        u.jsx(vp,{configId:"zzr6"}), vp being the shared grid (the wrapper renders u.jsx(vp,{...e,
        showViewSelector:!1})). A literal U+FEFF is now refused in every SPA file (none today), and the token gap
        treats it as whitespace. (b) import.meta.glob(...) with an escaped letter, in a file with no binding:
        the round-3 \u refusal applied only in binding files. Compiled to Object.assign({".../DataGrid.tsx":dE}),
        dE being the grid module namespace (DataGrid:vp). \u is now refused in every SPA file (none today). (c)
        import.meta /* c */ .glob(...): ImportMetaGlob read only whitespace between tokens. Compiled the same way.
        ImportMetaGlob now reads comments and U+FEFF between tokens, accepts ?. and refuses any computed
        import.meta[...] access (none in the SPA).
      - Q8 (element-tree peeling) stays residual 5, as before; the owner directive (runtime guard + documented
        residual when a scan cannot catch a shape with zero false positives) applies.
      MINOR item 8 (overclaims): reworded.
      - Guard remarks: the scan item no longer says the scan keeps every path to the grid going through a JSX mount
        of the pinned wrapper; it says the scan refuses the routes it knows of, in the spellings it recognises. The
        specifier and dynamic-load items say which form they read (keyword, whitespace, quote or "("), and a new item
        lists what the rules above do not read and how each is refused (R1-R5, U+FEFF, \u, import.meta). The fan-in
        paragraph and the "comments are not stripped" paragraph say the same for the library and for comments.
      - POML: the b2-r1 "CLOSED" is annotated in place. Note: section 1 row, G5 and section 7 were updated.
      CONFIRMED / CLEAN items 9-12, 14: no change needed. Item 13 (process): the integration hard gate
